      "text": "To determine the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\), we first need to find the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\).\n\nLet's perform the long division of 1 by 7:\n\n1. \\(1.000000 \\div 7\\)\n2. \\(1.000000\\) divided by \\(7\\) gives \\(0\\) with a remainder of \\(1\\).\n3. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(10\\).\n4. \\(10 \\div 7\\) gives \\(1\\) with a remainder of \\(3\\).\n5. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(30\\).\n6. \\(30 \\div 7\\) gives \\(4\\) with a remainder of \\(2\\).\n7. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(20\\).\n8. \\(20 \\div 7\\) gives \\(2\\) with a remainder of \\(6\\).\n9. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(60\\).\n10. \\(60 \\div 7\\) gives \\(8\\) with a remainder of \\(4\\).\n11. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(40\\).\n12. \\(40 \\div 7\\) gives \\(5\\) with a remainder of \\(5\\).\n13. Bring down the next \\(0\\), making it \\(50\\).\n14. \\(50 \\div 7\\) gives \\(7\\) with a remainder of \\(1\\).\n15. We see that the remainder \\(1\\) is repeating from this point onwards.\n\nThus, the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\n\nThe decimal repeats every 6 digits: \\(142857\\).\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating cycle. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\n\nThe remainder is \\(4\\), which means the 100th digit corresponds to the \\(4\\)-th digit in the repeating block \\(142857\\).\n\nThe digits in the repeating block are: \\(1, 4, 2, 8, 5, 7\\).\n\nThe 4th digit is \\(8\\).\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{8}\n\\]",
