      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\), we first need to determine the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\).\n\nWhen we divide 1 by 7, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{7} = 0.\\overline{142857}\n\\]\nThis means that the decimal expansion of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is a repeating decimal with the repeating block \"142857\". The block \"142857\" has a length of 6 digits.\n\nTo find the 100th digit after the decimal point, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating block. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6 (the length of the repeating block).\n\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\nThis tells us that 100 is 16 full cycles of the repeating block \"142857\" plus 4 more digits. Therefore, the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating block \"142857\".\n\nLooking at the block \"142857\", the 4th digit is 8.\n\nThus, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\(\\frac{1}{7}\\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
