      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\), we can start by determining the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\).\n\nFirst, we perform the long division of 1 by 7:\n\\[\n1 \\div 7 = 0.(142857)\n\\]\nThis tells us that the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is a repeating decimal with the repeating block \"142857\". The length of this repeating block is 6 digits.\n\nNext, we need to determine the position of the 100th digit within the repeating block. We do this by finding the remainder when 100 is divided by 6:\n\\[\n100 \\div 6 = 16 \\text{ remainder } 4\n\\]\nThis means that the 100th digit corresponds to the 4th digit in the repeating block \"142857\". The 4th digit in \"142857\" is 8.\n\nTherefore, the 100th digit after the decimal point of the decimal representation of \\( \\frac{1}{7} \\) is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).\n\nTo verify, we can count the digits:\n- The first digit after the decimal point is 1.\n- The second digit is 4.\n- The third digit is 2.\n- The fourth digit is 8.\n- The fifth digit is 5.\n- The sixth digit is 7.\n- And so on, repeating every 6 digits.\n\nSince 100 divided by 6 leaves a remainder of 4, the 100th digit is the 4th digit in the repeating block \"142857\", which is 8. The box with the final answer is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
